>> title: Massaman Curry
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: beef, coconut, thai, potatoes, curry
>> counters: Thai Kitchen, One Pot
>> aka: gaeng massaman, gaeng massamun, matsaman, mussaman curry, massaman beef, massaman
>> pairs-with: coconut-rice
>> servings: 6
>> time: 2 hr 30 min
>> step.1: crack the cream 5 min
>> step.2: fry the paste 4 min
>> step.3: simmer 90 min
>> step.4: simmer 25 min
>> step.5: season off the heat

Fry @coconut cream{1%cup}(240 mL) in a #dutch oven{} over medium heat for ~fry{5%min}, until it splits and beads its own oil.

Fry @massaman curry paste{1/2%cup}(120 g) into @&(~1)cracked cream{} for ~fry{4%min}, until the whole spice in it wakes up.

Simmer @beef chuck{2%lb}(900 g; in 1 1/2-in cubes), @coconut milk{28%oz}(800 mL; two cans), @cinnamon stick{1} and @bay leaves{2} with @&(~1)fried paste{}, covered and barely bubbling, for ~simmer{90%min}, until the beef gives under a spoon.

Simmer @Yukon Gold potatoes{1%lb}(450 g; in big chunks), @pearl onions{8}(peeled) and @roasted peanuts{1/2%cup} into @&(~1)curry{} for ~simmer{25%min}. This is the slow, gentle one — closer to a stew than to the other four, and it is the only curry here that is better the next day.

Season @&(~1)curry{} off the heat with @tamarind water{1/4%cup}(60 mL), @fish sauce{3%Tbs} and @palm sugar{2%Tbs}. It should land sweet, sour and salty in that order, with the heat somewhere behind all three.
